{
  "_readme": "Search terms for scripts/media_watch.py. Data, not code: edit here. Staff names are NOT listed here — they are read from our-team.html on every run, so a new hire is watched the day the team page lists them. Add people who are not on that page (a former director, a board chair) under staff_extra. Four kinds of source. (1) org_queries/work_terms go to Google News and Bing News: headline-level only. (2) outlet_feeds are read in full every run: `pages: N` fetches N pages of a WordPress feed (?paged=N) for an outlet whose feed holds only a few hours of stories; `read_pages: false` (YouTube) judges on the feed's own description and never fetches the page. (3) outlet_search asks each outlet's OWN search, which reaches full text and years back and does not care how deep its feed is: type wp-rest (WordPress REST, {name, base}; also searches each staff name unless \"staff\": false; \"timeout\": seconds for a slow backend, Maui Now takes ~30s a query), blox-rss (BLOX CMS, {name, base}), html (any site-search page, {name, url with {q}, optional page_url with {q} {qp} {n}, pages for a daily run and pages_deep for --deep}; result links are collected and each article read. Best-effort: results are relevance-ordered, some outlets stem the term, and pagination differs per site, so a feed is what guarantees new stories are seen). (4) podcast_terms go to Apple's podcast directory, which matches episode descriptions. alert_feeds are Google Alerts RSS feeds ({\"name\": ..., \"url\": ...}): google.com/alerts, create an alert for \"Hawaii Appleseed\", Deliver to: RSS feed, paste the link here — the only source that searches the whole web. staff_ambiguous lists names common enough that a bare match proves nothing (a candidate, a football player, \"Will White House\"); those need a job title from role_cues beside the name, or Appleseed in the article. A feed or search that starts failing prints a warning; add or drop outlets here. ignore_titles are regexes on the headline: a story that matches is never a mention. The default drops \"Candidate Q&A\" and the Star-Advertiser's \"2026 Election: <name>\" profiles because an election guide lists every candidate, so a staff member who ran appears on their rivals' pages too, in a public digest, for a reason that is not press coverage of the org; set it to [] to see them.",
